Diagnose "Input device not found" and accept --list-inputs indices

A field report from a Raspberry Pi 5 + Behringer UMC1820 install: output on
the card worked, --list-inputs showed the capture device at index 0, yet
every spelling of its name (plughw:, hw:, exact enumerated name, and the
listed index) failed with "Input device not found" when the daemon ran
under systemd.

Root cause of the confusion: cpal's ALSA backend only enumerates devices it
can open for capture at that instant, so a device that is busy (held by a
sound server or another capture process) or inaccessible (service user not
in the audio group) silently disappears from input enumeration - and our
error reported nothing but the requested name. Also, the listing prints
index numbers but device resolution never accepted them.

Changes:

- get_input_device now resolves an input device by exact name, then by
  --list-inputs index (a numeric device string), then by ALSA card match.
- InputError::DeviceNotFound carries the requested name, the device names
  that were enumerable at that moment, and (Linux) a direct ALSA
  capture-open probe of the requested name translated into an actionable
  diagnosis: EBUSY -> another process holds the capture side; EACCES/EPERM
  -> check the service user's audio group membership; ENOENT/ENODEV -> no
  such ALSA device. A name ALSA opens but cpal rejects points at an
  unsupported native format and suggests the plughw: form.
- --list-inputs prints an explanation instead of silence when zero devices
  are enumerable.
- libc added as a Linux-only dependency (already in the tree via alsa/cpal)
  for the errno constants.
- Docs: microphone-input.md, troubleshooting.md, and configuration.md cover
  index selection and the listed-but-not-found failure mode, including
  testing with sudo -u SERVICE_USER and fuser -v /dev/snd/*; CHANGELOG
  updated.

Tests written first (TDD): index parsing bounds, not-found message
construction (available list, empty-enumeration wording, probe inclusion),
Display of the structured error, and an end-to-end get_input_device call
against a nonexistent card asserting the Linux probe produces a diagnosis.

